---
title: User context
description: Store namespaced application state in the Spektral runtime.
---

Advanced adapter entrypoints expose a read/write API over the `user` store in `SpektralContext`:

```ts
import {
	setSpektralUserContext,
	useSpektralUserContext
} from 'spektral/svelte/advanced';
```

React also exports `useSetSpektralUserContext()` for writes from effects and event handlers. Vue and Svelte use `setSpektralUserContext(...)` directly.

## Read

```ts
const all = useSpektralUserContext();
const controls = useSpektralUserContext<Controls>('controls');
```

The first overload returns a `CurrentReadable` view of the complete namespace map. The second returns `CurrentReadable<T | undefined>` for one string or symbol namespace.

## Write

```ts
setSpektralUserContext(
	'controls',
	() => ({ enabled: true, speed: 1 }),
	{ existing: 'skip' }
);
```

The setter returns the effective value stored under the namespace.

| `existing` | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `'skip'` | Keep an existing value; do not evaluate a factory |
| `'replace'` | Store the new value |
| `'merge'` | Shallow-merge two plain records; otherwise replace |

The default is `'skip'`.

The complete namespace map is published as a frozen snapshot with no object prototype. This keeps
names such as `toString`, `constructor`, and `__proto__` independent from JavaScript prototype
properties. Treat snapshots as read-only and write through `setSpektralUserContext(...)` or the
`user` store. Merge mode accepts only records whose prototype is `Object.prototype` or `null`;
arrays, dates, functions, and class instances are replaced instead of merged.

Functions are treated as factories by default. Set `functionValue: 'value'` to store the function itself instead of calling it:

```ts
setSpektralUserContext('handler', callback, {
	existing: 'replace',
	functionValue: 'value'
});
```

## Use cases

Use separate namespaces for plugins or runtime subsystems that share a canvas but should not depend directly on each other. Use framework-native component state when data does not need to cross Spektral runtime boundaries.

All user-context functions require an active `FragCanvas` context. See [Hooks API](/docs/api-hooks-reference#advanced-user-context) for exact signatures.
